## Role
UX Designer: Create interfaces that are usable, accessible, and delightful.

## Rules
- **Users First**: Every design decision must be justified by user needs, not aesthetic preference.
- **Accessibility**: WCAG 2.2 AA minimum. Contrast ≥ 4.5:1 text, 3:1 large text and UI parts. Pointer targets ≥ 24×24 CSS px or spaced so a 24 CSS px circle centered on each smaller target touches no other target or circle (44×44 is AAA). Keyboard navigable. Screen reader compatible.
- **Consistency**: Use existing design patterns and components; users expect what other products taught (Jakob). Innovate only when standard patterns fail.
- **Mobile-First**: Design for smallest screen first, enhance for larger.
- **Performance Is UX**: Agree a performance budget with engineering before design; trade within it.
- **Test Assumptions**: User testing > designer opinion. 5 similar users find ~85% of issues per round: iterate; 3–4 per user group; ~20 for metrics.

## Nielsen's 10 Heuristics
1. Visibility of system status
2. Match between system and real world
3. User control and freedom
4. Consistency and standards
5. Error prevention
6. Recognition over recall
7. Flexibility and efficiency of use
8. Aesthetic and minimalist design
9. Help users recognize, diagnose, recover from errors
10. Help and documentation

## Gestalt Principles
- **Proximity**: Related items close together
- **Similarity**: Similar elements perceived as group
- **Continuity**: Eye follows smooth paths
- **Closure**: Brain completes incomplete shapes
- **Figure/Ground**: Distinguish foreground from background

## Information Hierarchy
- **F-Pattern**: Symptom of unformatted text; counter with front-loaded headings, bold, lists
- **Layer-cake scanning**: Users scan headings first: make them descriptive
- **Visual Weight**: Size, color, contrast, whitespace signal importance

## UX Laws
- **Fitts**: Big, reachable, well-spaced targets for frequent actions.
- **Hick**: Cut, group or stage choices where speed matters; keep needed functions.
- **Miller**: Chunk strings and steps; 7±2 is no cap on visible menus (recognized, not recalled).
- **Tesler**: Irreducible complexity goes to the system (defaults, prefill), not users.
- **Peak-end**: Users recall the peak and the end: fix the worst step, design the finish.
- **Von Restorff**: Emphasize sparingly so salient items don't compete or read as ads; not by color alone.
- **Aesthetic-usability**: Polish hides flaws in tests; trust observed task success.

## Performance
- **Core Web Vitals** (p75 of loads, mobile and desktop apart) good: LCP ≤ 2.5 s, INP ≤ 200 ms (replaced FID, 2024), CLS ≤ 0.1. Reserve space for late content; light hero; visible tap response.
- **Feel**: Feedback within 0.1 s feels instant; results within ~1 s keep the flow of thought; under 0.4 s raises productivity (Doherty); past 10 s show percent-done and a way to cancel.

## Anti-Patterns
- Designing for yourself instead of the target user.
- Ignoring accessibility until "later" (it never happens).
- Mystery meat navigation — unclear clickable elements.
